WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:39.000
It's that time of year when parents and other caregivers race to find the hottest toys and stock up on classic favorites. From poppets to Pokemon, from squishmallows to Legos. Toys might seem frivolous, but to psychologists, they're a subject for serious study. Researchers have long known that children learn best through play. Play is how kids stretch their brains and develop crucial cognitive, social, and other skills. toys are a fundamental part of playtime. But what makes something a good toy? Indeed, what makes something a toy at all?
每到这个时节，家长和其他看护人都会争相寻找最热门的玩具，并囤积经典的畅销款。从按压泡泡玩具到宝可梦，从毛绒公仔到乐高，玩具看起来似乎微不足道，但对心理学家而言，它们是一个严肃的研究课题。研究人员早就知道，儿童在玩耍中学习效果最好。玩耍是孩子们拓展思维、发展关键认知、社交及其他技能的方式。玩具是玩耍的基本组成部分。但什么才算是一个好玩具？事实上，到底什么才算是玩具？

2
00:00:39.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Why do some toys stand the test of time, while others are just a one-season wonder? What's the difference between fun and educational toys? Why do we assign gender appropriateness to some toys and not to others? How do kids' toy preferences change as they age, and how has technology change what toys are and the way kids play with toys. Welcome to Speaking of Psychology,
为什么有些玩具经得起时间的考验，而有些却只是昙花一现？有趣玩具和益智玩具之间有什么区别？为什么我们给某些玩具贴上性别标签，而对其他玩具则不然？随着年龄增长，孩子们的玩具偏好如何改变？科技又是如何改变玩具的定义以及孩子们玩玩具的方式的？欢迎收听《心理学谈话》，

3
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:26.000
the flagship podcast of the American Psychological Association that examines the links between psychological science and everyday life. I'm Kim Mills. We have two guests today. Our first is Dr. Barry Kudrowitz, an associate professor and director of product design at the University of Minnesota Twin Cities,
这是美国心理学会的旗舰播客，旨在探讨心理科学与日常生活之间的联系。我是主持人金·米尔斯。今天我们邀请到了两位嘉宾。第一位是巴里·库德罗维茨博士，他是明尼苏达大学双城分校的产品设计副教授兼主任，

4
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:58.000
where he teaches classes in toy product design. Dr. Kudrowitz has a PhD in mechanical engineering from the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, where he studied creativity, humor, and idea generation, and he has studied how play can fuel creativity and innovation. In addition to his academic career, he's also a toy designer. Highlights of his work include the Katsup Krapper, an anthropomorphic roller skating ketchup bottle that he designed as a grad student, and the Nerf Adam Blaster, a Nerf toy sold by Hasbro more than five years.
他在那里教授玩具产品设计课程。库德罗维茨博士拥有麻省理工学院机械工程博士学位，研究方向包括创造力、幽默感和创意生成，他还研究过玩耍如何激发创造力和创新。除了学术生涯外，他也是一名玩具设计师。他的代表作包括他在研究生时期设计的拟人化溜冰番茄酱瓶“Katsup Krapper”，以及孩之宝销售超过五年的“Nerf Adam Blaster”发射器。

5
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:41.000
Our second guest is Dr. Doris Bergen, a distinguished professor emerita of educational psychology at Miami University in Oxford, Ohio. Dr. Bergen began her career as a pre-kindergarten and elementary school teacher. And over her long academic career, she has explored the many ways that play is crucial to children's development. She has studied play and humor in early and middle childhood, the effects of technology enhanced toys, and cross-cultural considerations in play among many other topics. Dr. Bergen has authored or edited more than a dozen books, including her most recent, the Handbook of Developmentally Appropriate Toys, which was published in March 2021. Thank you both for joining us today.
我们的第二位嘉宾是多丽丝·伯根博士，她是俄亥俄州牛津市迈阿密大学教育心理学的杰出荣誉教授。伯根博士的职业生涯始于学前班和小学教师。在她漫长的学术生涯中，她探索了玩耍对儿童发展的诸多关键作用。她研究过幼儿和童年中期的玩耍与幽默、科技增强型玩具的影响，以及玩耍中的跨文化考量等诸多课题。伯根博士撰写或编辑了十几本书，包括她于2021年3月出版的最新著作《适龄玩具手册》。感谢两位今天来到我们的节目。

6
00:02:41.000 --> 00:03:05.000
Thanks for having me. God be here. All right. So let's start with a broad question, which we usually do. What is the purpose of toys and what makes something a good toy or make something a toy at all? For example, can a stick or a rock be a toy? Actually, toys have existed for a long, long time. You can even find them in early sites.
谢谢邀请。很高兴能来。好的。那么，让我们从一个通常会问的宏大问题开始。玩具的目的是什么？什么才算是一个好玩具，或者说到底什么才算是玩具？例如，一根棍子或一块石头能成为玩具吗？事实上，玩具已经存在了很长很长的时间。你甚至可以在早期的遗址中发现它们。

7
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:49.000
And so some of the typical toys that children still play with today, like dolls and wheel toys, things that sort, have been around for a long time. But I would say that the purpose of toys, in my view, is to enhance children's play, because children will play, even if there aren't any toys. They'll find sticks or they'll find rocks or they'll find other things to use in their pretend play and in their gameplay. But toys are a means of enhancing the play and making it more creative and often more elaborative.
因此，一些孩子们今天仍在玩的典型玩具，比如玩偶和带轮子的玩具，这类东西已经存在很久了。但我认为，玩具的目的在于增强儿童的玩耍体验，因为即使没有玩具，孩子们也会玩耍。他们会找棍子、石头或其他东西来用于假装游戏和游戏活动。但玩具是一种增强玩耍、使其更具创造性且往往更丰富多彩的手段。

8
00:03:49.000 --> 00:04:32.000
Yeah, I agree. Toys are a tool for play. And, you know, there's a difference between a toy and a toy product. There are certain toys that are designed by people to be intended to be used for play. And then there's things that are not designed, you know, intended to be used for play that children still play with, like cardboard boxes and spoons and rakes and stuff, right? There's still toys, but maybe they're not toy products. And many toy products are designed by people who have ideas about what they think is good for children to play with.
是的，我同意。玩具是玩耍的工具。而且，你知道，玩具和玩具产品之间是有区别的。有些玩具是人们专门设计出来用于玩耍的。而有些东西并非为了玩耍而设计，但孩子们依然会玩，比如纸箱、勺子、耙子之类的东西，对吧？它们仍然是玩具，但可能不是“玩具产品”。许多玩具产品是由那些对“什么适合孩子玩”有自己想法的人设计的。

9
00:04:32.000 --> 00:05:09.000
Maybe they think they'll learn particular things from them. They have different expectations about the toys. And I would say a really good toy is one that doesn't do everything itself, but has the child do it. Unfortunately, I think some toys designed do too much, and there isn't really even room for children to do very much with them. And so those toys typically don't have very lasting value. Maybe kids will play with them for a while, learn what they do,
也许他们认为孩子能从中学习到特定的东西。他们对玩具抱有不同的期望。而我会说，一个真正的好玩具，不是那种自己能做所有事情的玩具，而是能让孩子参与其中的玩具。遗憾的是，我认为有些玩具设计得太“全能”了，孩子几乎没有发挥的空间。因此，这些玩具通常没有持久的价值。也许孩子们会玩上一阵子，了解它们的功能，

10
00:05:09.000 --> 00:06:01.000
and then they'll probably turn them into something else and use them in a different way or maybe discard them altogether. And are those things that you think about, Dr. Kudrowitz, when you're designing toys or teaching people how to design toys? I mean, I'm thinking about them, but not necessarily the students. And even when we do playtesting, it's not necessarily even the children that we playtest are thinking about those things. Oftentimes, it's what is trendy or popular that they might gravitate to. but I also think that what makes the toy a good toy is that it can be used in a variety of different contexts and it doesn't die. You know, you can use it for generations, for example, or in multiple different ways of playing.
然后他们可能会把它们变成别的东西，以不同的方式使用，或者干脆丢弃。库德罗维茨博士，当您在设计玩具或教别人如何设计玩具时，会考虑这些吗？我的意思是，我在考虑这些，但学生们不一定。即使我们在进行游戏测试时，我们测试的孩子也不一定在考虑这些。通常，他们会被潮流或热门的东西所吸引。但我同样认为，一个好玩具的特质在于它可以在多种不同的情境下使用，而且不会“过时”。你知道，它可以被几代人使用，或者以多种不同的方式玩耍。

11
00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:28.000
You know, like the cardboard box is a great example. We actually have a box in our dining room right now from a couch, but it's been played with by the children for months now and we left this giant box in the dining room. And they play with that more than, you know, all the toys we've got in so far. It's a fort. It's a rocket ship. They're drawing all over the walls. They're cutting out holes. And that was free.
你知道，纸箱就是一个很好的例子。我们餐厅里现在就有一个装沙发的箱子，但孩子们已经玩了好几个月了，我们把这个巨大的箱子留在餐厅里。他们玩这个箱子的次数比我们买的所有玩具都要多。它是一个堡垒，是一艘火箭飞船。他们在墙上乱涂乱画，挖出洞来。而且那是免费的。

12
00:06:28.000 --> 00:07:08.000
And it's compostable. And I think it's fantastic. There are sort of different kinds of play, you know, of course, and different kind of toys facilitate different kinds. If you're thinking of just kind of exploratory play or, manipulative play, that's one kind of toy that'll work for that. If you're looking at pretend play, there's a whole range of other kinds of toys that are really facilitative of pretense. And then, of course, there's game play of all different kinds and very different kinds of toys for that. And there's certainly some overlap among those.
而且它还可以堆肥。我觉得这太棒了。当然，玩耍有不同的种类，不同的玩具促进不同类型的玩耍。如果你考虑的是探索性游戏或操作性游戏，那会有相应的玩具。如果你关注的是假装游戏，还有一系列其他类型的玩具能很好地促进这种想象。当然，还有各种各样的游戏活动，也有非常不同的玩具。这些类别之间肯定会有一些重叠。

13
00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:52.000
But the type of play that the child is going to do, if it's active play, quiet play, or of various sorts will usually need, or at least there'll be an expectation that different kinds of toys will meet the needs for different kinds of play. So a lot of parents think that toys come in basically two categories. And Dr. Bergen, you just described several categories, not just two, but for parents, they think they're educational toys, like those that are designed to teach science or math or reading, and then there are toys that are for fun, which covers pretty much everything else. Is that a valid distinction? And is there a difference between a fun toy and an educational toy?
但孩子将要进行的玩耍类型，无论是主动玩耍、安静玩耍还是其他各种形式，通常都需要，或者至少人们会期望不同的玩具能满足不同玩耍的需求。所以很多家长认为玩具基本上分为两类。伯根博士，您刚才描述了几个类别，不只是两类，但家长们认为有益智玩具，比如那些旨在教授科学、数学或阅读的玩具，还有就是为了好玩的玩具，这几乎涵盖了其他所有东西。这种区分有效吗？好玩的玩具和益智玩具之间有区别吗？

14
00:07:52.000 --> 00:08:17.000
Should there be? I mean, going back to the very first thing we talked about, the primary purpose of a toy is play. And if the primary purpose is to teach something, it may not be a very good toy. But, you know, that being said, play is how we learn. That's how we've learned skills growing up, as we learned developmental skills, cognitive skills, physical skills.
应该有区别吗？我的意思是，回到我们谈论的第一点，玩具的主要目的是玩耍。如果主要目的是为了教学，那它可能不是一个好玩具。但是，话虽如此，玩耍是我们学习的方式。这就是我们在成长过程中学习技能的方式，我们学习发展技能、认知技能、身体技能。

15
00:08:17.000 --> 00:09:08.000
And so you could also argue that no matter what kind of play, if you're engaging in play, you could argue that you're learning something. And so it's a little bit hard to separate those two, but I don't necessarily like the idea of a toy being primarily designed to be teaching you something. I would agree. I think that's maybe more of a marketing distinction than it is a toy distinction, because I think children learn from every type of play and from every type of toy. They may not be learning a specific thing like letters or whatever the toy maker thought they would do. But they also learn many other
所以你也可以说，无论进行哪种玩耍，只要你参与其中，就可以说你在学习。因此，要把这两者分开有点困难，但我并不一定喜欢那种主要为了教学而设计的玩具。我同意。我认为这更多是一种营销上的区分，而不是玩具本身的区分，因为我认为孩子们从每一种玩耍和每一种玩具中都能学到东西。他们可能学到的不是字母之类的特定知识，也不是玩具制造商所预想的内容。但他们也学到了许多其他

16
00:09:08.000 --> 00:10:10.000
social and physical and intellectual skills from just about any toy. So I think the distinction educational toys is primarily a distinction for parents and for teachers and people who think that, oh, if it says educational, it's going to have a greater educational factor. The other way that educational toy could be seen is if there's a specific kind of teaching that the adult wants a child to have. And unfortunately, sometimes those kind of toys that are very specifically designed to teach a certain thing are either not used very often by the child or the child transforms them in some way into pretend or something else. And they don't exactly use them in the same. same way that the person who designed them thought they would use them.
社交、身体和智力技能，几乎从任何玩具中都能学到。所以我认为“益智玩具”这个区分主要是为了家长、老师以及那些认为“哦，如果上面写着益智，它就会有更大的教育价值”的人而设的。另一种看待益智玩具的方式是，如果成年人希望孩子掌握某种特定的教学内容。遗憾的是，有时那些专门设计用来教授特定知识的玩具，要么孩子不怎么用，要么孩子以某种方式将其转化为假装游戏或其他用途。他们并没有完全按照设计者预想的方式去使用。

17
00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:45.000
And I think that's an amazing thing that kids can do. I mean, I've known parents, for example, who've decided that they never want their children to have a toy gun. And somehow, kids will figure out how to make something into a gun. If they want to play with guns, they're going to have a make-believe gun, right? I mean, it's just the creative end of childhood. Dr. Cudrowitz, you were quoted in a recent article talking about the fact that toy makers worry that kids are losing interest in toys at a younger age than they use. to? Is that actually happening? And if it is, why do you think so? So this is related to the concept of age compression. There's probably other terms related
我认为孩子们能做到这一点非常了不起。我的意思是，我认识一些家长，他们决定永远不让孩子玩玩具枪。但不知何故，孩子们总能想办法把东西变成枪。如果他们想玩枪，他们就会制造一把假想的枪，对吧？我的意思是，这就是童年的创造力。库德罗维茨博士，您在最近的一篇文章中被引用，谈到玩具制造商担心孩子们在比过去更小的年龄就对玩具失去了兴趣。这真的在发生吗？如果是，您认为为什么？这与“年龄压缩”的概念有关。可能还有其他相关的术语

18
00:10:45.000 --> 00:11:36.000
to this. We talked to Doris about that, but it seems like kids are playing with the stuff that the older kids were playing with in the past. And this may be more of an artifact of technology advancing to the degree that things that we used to play with in the past were I don't want to say dumb, but they didn't have high tech inside because that was expensive. And in today's society, you can put LEDs in anything and it's relatively low cost. You could put motors in things and processors and there's computers in everything, you know, in my pocket right now. And it's not that much more expensive in today's society. And so a lot of the things that children are playing with today are not,
我们和多丽丝谈过这个，但看起来孩子们正在玩过去大孩子才玩的东西。这可能更多是科技进步带来的结果，即我们过去玩的东西——我不想说它们很笨，但它们内部没有高科技，因为那很昂贵。而在今天的社会，你可以在任何东西里装上LED灯，成本相对较低。你可以给东西装上电机和处理器，每样东西里都有电脑，你知道，我现在口袋里就有一台。在今天的社会，这并没有贵多少。所以孩子们今天玩的很多东西，

19
00:11:36.000 --> 00:12:30.000
necessarily video games, but they're more like technology. And in the market space, some of those things kind of get lumped into consumer electronics and not necessarily in the toy aisle. And so, again, it kind of goes back to maybe marketing in some ways, but also you're not necessarily seeing kids play with the same toys as they were years ago. And there's a lot more screens and digital things in their hands. As a long-time observer of play and interviewing of adults' memories of their childhood pay, I definitely agree with Barry that the way play, the time and the experiences children have in play now has changed quite a bit.
不一定是电子游戏，但它们更像是科技产品。在市场上，其中一些东西被归类为消费电子产品，而不一定在玩具区。所以，这在某种程度上又回到了营销问题，但你也不一定能看到孩子们像几年前那样玩同样的玩具了。他们手中有了更多的屏幕和数字产品。作为一名长期的玩耍观察者，并采访过成年人对童年玩耍的记忆，我绝对同意巴里的观点，即玩耍的方式、孩子们现在玩耍的时间和体验已经发生了很大的变化。

20
00:12:30.000 --> 00:13:25.000
When you think back to your own playtime, or certainly many other people who have either interviewed. They recall long periods of time where they pretty much could decide what they wanted to do with the time. And the kind of play materials they had were ones that they created, they made the toy talk, or they made the toy walk, or they did whatever it was with their own imagination. And now, so many of the toys, the toy does everything. There's really not much for the child to do. And so that toy is not going to be nearly as important in terms of children's development or their interest level, because if you're just, there's 10 buttons to push and
当你回想自己的玩耍时光，或者采访过的许多其他人时。他们回忆起很长一段时间里，他们几乎可以决定自己想怎么利用这段时间。他们拥有的玩耍材料是他们自己创造的，他们让玩具说话，或者让玩具走路，或者用他们自己的想象力做任何事情。而现在，太多的玩具，玩具本身就做了一切。孩子真的没什么可做的了。所以那个玩具在儿童发展或兴趣水平方面就不会那么重要了，因为如果你只是，有10个按钮要按，

21
00:13:25.000 --> 00:14:17.000
you've pushed all 10 buttons, and then that's really the end of, what you can do with the toy, usually that what you see the child do is either put it away and not look at it anymore or pick it up and pretend it's something altogether different and use imagination and do something else with it. So I do think that that change and certainly that idea that experiences have gone down to lower levels is not just, related to toys, but it's pervasive across a lot of other things for children today. But I do think I've often thought the people designing the technology are the ones that are having the playful time because they're the ones that get to figure out what to do with it
你按完了所有10个按钮，那通常就是你能用这个玩具做的所有事情了。你看到孩子做的通常要么是把它收起来不再看它，要么是把它拿起来假装它是完全不同的东西，发挥想象力做点别的。所以我确实认为这种变化，当然还有那种体验水平下降的想法，不仅仅与玩具有关，而且在当今儿童的许多其他事物中都很普遍。但我确实经常认为，设计这些技术的人才是真正享受玩耍时光的人，因为他们是那些能弄清楚怎么玩它的人，

22
00:14:17.000 --> 00:15:12.000
and so on. So it's the grownups who are coming up with these ideas. The kids aren't asking for the toys to be, yeah. So are you recommending that parents give their kids like dolls that don't do anything except sit there? They still make those. I know. One of the things that concerns me a great deal is if you remember back to your childhood, maybe all summer, you didn't have anything to do except just figure out what to play with yourself. Now children's lives are very structured, both after school and in summer. And so they don't necessarily have those long periods of time where they can really create their own play world. So that's a very, very different thing that we see now. So let me ask a kind of a developmental question. Maybe this is for Dr. Bergen,
等等。所以是成年人在想出这些点子。孩子们并没有要求玩具变成这样。那么，您是建议家长给孩子买那种除了坐在那儿什么都不做的玩偶吗？他们还在生产那种玩偶。我知道。让我非常担心的一点是，如果你回想自己的童年，也许整个夏天，你除了自己想办法玩什么之外，什么事都没有。现在孩子们的生活非常有条理，无论是课后还是暑假。所以他们不一定有那些可以真正创造自己玩耍世界的长段时间。所以这是我们现在看到的非常、非常不同的情况。那么让我问一个发展方面的问题。也许这是问伯根博士的，

